.TH TR 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
tr \- translate, squeeze, and/or delete characters
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tr
.RB [ \-c | \-C ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.I string1 string2
.br
.B tr
.B \-s
.RB [ \-c | \-C ]
.I string1
.br
.B tr
.B \-d
.RB [ \-c | \-C ]
.I string1
.br
.B tr
.B \-ds
.RB [ \-c | \-C ]
.I string1 string2
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tr
reads characters from standard input, translates, squeezes, and/or
deletes them according to
.I string1
and (when present)
.IR string2 ,
and writes the result to standard output. There are no file operands
\(em input is always standard input, output always standard output.
Exactly one of the four combinations shown in SYNOPSIS is accepted;
any other combination of
.BR \-c / \-C ", " \-d ", "
and
.B \-s
with operands is a usage error, including the wrong number of
operands for the combination requested.
.PP
With no
.BR \-d ,
each character read that appears in
.I string1
(as expanded \(em see OPERANDS) is replaced with the character at the
corresponding position of
.I string2
(also as expanded). If
.IR string2 's
expansion is shorter than
.IR string1 's
(after any
.B \-c / \-C
complement is applied), it is padded out to the same length by
repeating its own last character.
.PP
With
.BR \-d ,
each character read that appears in
.I string1
is deleted from the output instead.
.PP
With
.BR \-s ,
after any deletion or translation has already taken place, a run of
two or more consecutive identical characters in the output is
replaced by a single occurrence, but only for a character that
appears in the applicable set: \fIstring2\fR's expansion when a
.I string2
operand is present (plain translate\-with\-squeeze, and
.BR \-ds ),
or \fIstring1\fR's expansion (after
.BR \-c / \-C )
otherwise (\fB\-s\fR alone).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c
Complement the set of characters named by
.IR string1 :
operate on every byte value 0\-255 that is
.I not
in
.IR string1 ,
in ascending numeric order.
.TP
.B \-C
Accepted as an exact synonym of
.BR \-c .
POSIX distinguishes complementing by character value
.RB ( \-c )
from complementing in the current collating sequence
.RB ( \-C ),
but this implementation has no multibyte\-aware collation order
beyond plain ascending numeric order, so the two are identical here.
.TP
.B \-d
Delete every character read that appears in
.I string1
(after any
.B \-c / \-C
complement), instead of translating it.
.TP
.B \-s
Squeeze runs of repeated output characters down to one occurrence;
see DESCRIPTION for exactly which characters this applies to in each
combination.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I string1
The source character set: every character read that matches an entry
in
.I string1
(after
.BR \-c / \-C ,
if given) is a candidate for deletion, translation, or squeezing.
.TP
.I string2
The replacement character set, required for plain translate and for
.BR \-ds ,
optional for
.B \-s
alone, and refused for plain
.B \-d
(without
.BR \-s ).
.PP
Both
.I string1
and
.I string2
share the same grammar, expanded once into a flat list of characters
before translation begins:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
An ordinary character stands for itself.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR \e\e ", " \ea ", " \eb ", " \ef ", " \en ", " \er ", " \et ", "
and
.B \ev
are the named backslash escapes. \fB\e\fIddd\fR is one to three
octal digits, giving that byte value. A backslash followed by
anything else stands for the literal character after it (rather than
being refused, unlike most other ambiguous constructs in this
grammar); a trailing backslash with nothing after it stands for
itself.
.IP \(bu 2
.IB c \- c2
is an ascending range of characters from
.I c
through
.IR c2 ,
inclusive.
.I c2
must not sort before
.IR c ;
a descending range is a usage error, not silently reversed.
.IP \(bu 2
.BI [: name :]
is one of the twelve POSIX character classes:
.BR alnum ", " alpha ", " blank ", " cntrl ", " digit ", " graph ", "
.BR lower ", " print ", " punct ", " space ", " upper ,
and
.BR xdigit .
Every byte value 0\-255 classified true by that class is included, in
ascending order.
.IP \(bu 2
.BI [= c =]
is the equivalence class of
.IR c .
With no collating\-sequence database beyond byte value, the
equivalence class of a character is just that character itself.
.IP \(bu 2
.IB x *n
(only valid within
.I string2 )
repeats the character
.I x
.I n
times.
.I n
may be omitted or given as 0, meaning "however many copies are needed
to make \fIstring2\fR's total expansion as long as \fIstring1\fR's";
at most one such construct is honored per string, and a second one is
a usage error. A leading\-zero
.I n
is interpreted as octal, otherwise decimal.
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B 1
A memory allocation failed while building the complement of
.I string1
(with
.BR \-c / \-C ).
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: an invalid option, the wrong number or combination of
.I string1
/
.I string2
operands for the
.BR \-c / \-C / \-d / \-s
combination given, an empty
.IR string2 ,
a
.I string1
/
.I string2
grammar violation (an unterminated
.B [: :]
or
.B [= =]
construct, an unknown class name, a descending range, an
.B [ \fIx\fB*\fIn\fB]
construct in
.IR string1 ,
or a second such construct in the same string), or a memory
allocation failure while expanding
.I string1
or
.IR string2 .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cut (1),
.BR fold (1),
.BR paste (1)
